---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * Every sentence here must be TRUE of the site as it exists today, not a boilerplate policy
 * describing a product that has not been built. Today the site is a set of static pages with no
 * account system, no forms, no analytics, no advertising and no third-party script of any kind.
 * Saying more than that would be a fabricated claim in the other direction (AGENTS.md section 1.1),
 * and a policy that overstates collection is as misleading as one that understates it.
 *
 * The commitments in "What this site will never collect" are not aspirational: they are binding
 * invariants from AGENTS.md section 2 that the build is held to.
 *
 * This page must be rewritten — not appended to — the moment any account, alert, analytics or
 * advertising surface ships. docs/agents/ROSTER.md section 3 assigns the policy pages to
 * trust-compliance-officer; this is a pre-Phase-11 baseline added under direct owner instruction
 * because the site had no privacy page at all.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const title = 'Privacy — DelayPilot'
const description =
  'What DelayPilot collects today (almost nothing), and what it is built never to collect.'

const lastUpdated = '29 August 2026'
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} nav="privacy">
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      What this site collects today, stated plainly — and the things it is built never to collect.
    </p>
    <p class="meta">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What this site collects today</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>No account. No forms. No tracking.</strong> As of the date above, DelayPilot is a set
        of static informational pages. There is no sign-up, no login, no flight lookup, and no form of
        any kind. The site sets no cookies, runs no analytics, serves no advertising, and loads no third-party
        script.
      </p>

      <p>
        The site is served through Cloudflare. Like any web host, Cloudflare records standard server
        request logs — including IP address, user agent, requested path and timestamp — and retains
        them under its own policy for security and reliability purposes. We do not add to those
        logs, query them for analytics, or combine them with anything else.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site will never collect</h2>

      <p>
        These are binding rules on the product, not preferences. They hold as new features ship:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No booking reference.</strong> DelayPilot will never ask for a PNR or record locator.
          It is not an input, not a database column, not a URL parameter, and not a log field.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No identity documents.</strong> Never a passport, government ID, Known Traveler Number,
          redress number, card number, or airline account credential.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No access to your accounts.</strong> DelayPilot will never read your email inbox, sign
          in to an airline on your behalf, or submit a claim, refund request, or rebooking for you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No personal detail in logs or analytics.</strong> No email address, display name, itinerary
          detail, receipt text, notification payload, or raw IP address is written into application logs,
          analytics events, page titles, or URLs.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>When this page changes</h2>

      <p>
        This page will be rewritten before — not after — any feature that collects personal data
        goes live. That rewrite will say exactly what is collected, why, how long it is kept, and
        how to have it deleted. The date at the top is the record of when the description last
        matched the site.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>

      <p>
        Questions about this page can be raised through the project's public repository. No contact
        form is offered here, because a form that collects an email address would contradict the
        section above.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
